 @dan_loeb  At 30 fps you already have 33.33 ms frametimes. At that point, increasing FPS with only 400% (120 fps) reduces frametimes to 8.333333 MS. Yes 20/30 ms difference is huge in competitive games, but for everything else, even if you get 25ms, it equalizes itself pretty good and in return you get a smooth experience that's enjoyable.

If you want to go from 150fps to 300fps, the difference decreases a lot. Honestly, frame generation was made to make older/lower hardware run games with higher graphics requirements and to allow users to get increased fidelity at the same performance.

Juding by the emphasis on DLSS/Compute, it may be possible that for those who want no AI/DLSS/Framegen, this generation is a lackluster upgrade and can be disregarded.

