 @Polyh3dron  that is literally impossible. As the stuttering comes from the CPU not the GPU. It works like this: engine wants to send a draw call to the GPU, GPU tries to shade but finds no pipeline cache for the object, GPU report to the driver, driver issues the CPU to compile the entire pipeline for that shader.
If this process takes longer than your desired framerate you will notice a stutter, because the game itself stops, on the CPU. This is outside of the control of Nvidia. Stuttering only occurs on low level APIs like DirectX12 and Vulkan where Developers EXPLICITLY say "ok, I will deal with everything, trust me". Devs that dont want to do this still ship DX11, you wont find stuttering there as there are no pipelines, there is a "premade" shader so to say.
Again, this is out of Nvidias, AMDs or Intels control, its the devs that are stupid and there is no way around it...

