 @dmytrosoboliev935  pure shader rendering vs DLSS is not superior in every way.  And even if you don’t want to upscale, there is a ton of useful information in previous frames that can be used to improve the current frame (DLAA).  It doesn’t make sense to throw all that info away.

DLSS can result in some detail loss, particularly in the interior of textures, as well as ghosting, but DLSS can also significantly improve temporal instability in an image - things like rails that have sunlight reflecting off them.  

Personally I notice flickering / noise from temporal instability more than a somewhat minor loss to inter-texture detail, so I’ll turn on DLAA or DLSS quality even if a game has high frames without it.    Each game engine performs differently so it depends on the implementation but

